2026-11-17|Minimum Changes to Make K Semi-palindromes

Question:
Given a string `s` and an integer `k`, partition `s` into exactly `k` substrings such that every substring becomes a semi-palindrome.

Return the minimum number of character changes required.

Important condition:

\- Every substring must have a length of at least 2 because a string of length 1 does not have a valid proper divisor.

Approach:
We will divide the solution into two parts.

Part 1: Precompute the minimum cost for every substring

1\. Create a 2D array:

`cost[i][j]`

It represents the minimum number of character changes required to convert the substring `s[i...j]` into a semi-palindrome.

2\. Consider every possible substring.

&#x20;  Let its length be:

`len = j - i + 1`

3\. Find all proper divisors of its length.

A divisor `d` is valid if:

- `len % d == 0`
- `d < len`

&#x20;  For example, if the substring length is 6, possible divisors are:

`d = 1, 2, 3`

4\. For each valid divisor `d`, divide the substring into groups.

Each group contains characters whose positions differ by `d`.

&#x20;  For example:

`s = "abcabc"`

With `d = 3`:

- Group 1 = `"aa"`
- Group 2 = `"bb"`
- Group 3 = `"cc"`

5\. Calculate the changes required to make every group a palindrome.

&#x20;  To convert a group into a palindrome, compare characters from both ends.

- If both characters are equal, no change is required.
- If they are different, one character must be changed.

&#x20;  Therefore, every mismatched pair contributes exactly 1 operation.

6\. Add the changes required by all groups.

&#x20;  Repeat this process for every valid divisor.

&#x20;  The minimum cost among all divisors becomes:

`cost[i][j]`

Part 2: Dynamic Programming to divide the string into exactly k substrings

Now that we know the conversion cost of every substring, we can use DP to find the minimum total cost.

1\. Define the DP state:

`dp[p][i]`

It represents the minimum number of changes required to divide the first `i` characters of the string into exactly `p` semi-palindromic substrings.

2\. Initialize the DP array.

&#x20;  Initially, all states are set to infinity.

&#x20;  The base condition is:

`dp[0][0] = 0`

&#x20;  This means that zero characters can be divided into zero substrings with zero operations.

3\. Try every possible number of substrings.

For each `p` from 1 to `k`, consider every possible ending position `i`.

4\. Try every possible starting position `j` for the last substring.

&#x20;  The last substring will be:

`s[j...i-1]`

&#x20;  Its conversion cost is:

`cost[j][i-1]`

5\. Apply the DP transition:

\\[ dp[p][i] = \min\_{j} \left(dp[p-1][j] + cost[j][i-1]\right) \\]

&#x20;  Here:

- `dp[p-1][j]` represents the minimum cost of dividing the previous `j` characters into `p-1` substrings.
- `cost[j][i-1]` represents the cost of converting the last substring into a semi-palindrome.

6\. Make sure every substring has at least 2 characters.

&#x20;  Therefore, the starting position must satisfy:

`j <= i - 2`

7\. After processing all states, return:

`dp[k][n]`

This gives the minimum changes required to divide the complete string into exactly `k` semi-palindromic substrings.

Solution:

```

class Solution {
public:
    int minimumChanges(string s, int k) {

        int n = s.size();

        const int INF = 1e9;

        // Part 1: Precompute the cost of every substring
        vector<vector<int>> cost(n, vector<int>(n, INF));

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {

                int len = j - i + 1;

                // Try every proper divisor
                for (int d = 1; d < len; d++) {

                    if (len % d != 0) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    int changes = 0;

                    // Check each group formed using divisor d
                    for (int r = 0; r < d; r++) {

                        int groupSize = len / d;

                        // Compare symmetric characters
                        for (int x = 0; x < groupSize / 2; x++) {

                            int left = i + r + x * d;

                            int right = i + r +
                                        (groupSize - 1 - x) * d;

                            if (s[left] != s[right]) {
                                changes++;
                            }
                        }
                    }

                    cost[i][j] = min(cost[i][j], changes);
                }
            }
        }

        // Part 2: Dynamic Programming
        vector<vector<int>> dp(
            k + 1,
            vector<int>(n + 1, INF)
        );

        // Base condition
        dp[0][0] = 0;

        for (int p = 1; p <= k; p++) {

            // At least 2 characters are needed per substring
            for (int i = 2 * p; i <= n; i++) {

                // Starting position of the last substring
                for (int j = 2 * (p - 1); j <= i - 2; j++) {

                    if (dp[p - 1][j] == INF) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    dp[p][i] = min(
                        dp[p][i],
                        dp[p - 1][j] + cost[j][i - 1]
                    );
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[k][n];
    }
};

```

